📗
  🚇 What a connection is read from and written to. The connection loop
  knows nothing else about where its bytes come from, so the same loop serves
  a socket and an in-memory test.

  Time is part of the transport on purpose: a test transport can make a
  timeout pass instantly by moving its own clock.

  Statuses, for reads and writes alike: 0 fine, 1 timed out, 2 the peer
  closed, 3 failed.
📗
🐊 🚇 🍇
  📗 Reads up to *max* bytes, waiting at most *timeoutMs*. Empty unless 🌡 is 0. 📗
  ❗️ 👂 max 🔢 timeoutMs 🔢 ➡️ 📇

  📗 Status of the last read. 📗
  ❗️ 🌡 ➡️ 🔢

  📗 Writes all of *data*, waiting at most *timeoutMs* each time the peer stops accepting. Returns a status. 📗
  ❗️ 💬 data 📇 timeoutMs 🔢 ➡️ 🔢

  📗 Closes, first giving the peer up to *lingerMs* to read what was written. 📗
  ❗️ 🚪 lingerMs 🔢

  📗 Address of the peer. 📗
  ❗️ 🌐 ➡️ 🔡

  📗 The current time in milliseconds. Only differences mean anything. 📗
  ❗️ ⏱ ➡️ 🔢
